{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nThe original macOS CI incident was observed after pytest passed: owner-nonwritable `.rebar/run_snapshots` trees made recursive basetemp removal fail. Commit `7d908fbbc6` removed the rejected RAM-disk workflow step and added cleanup only to `tests/unit/workflow/test_snapshot.py:19-24`. The cleanup invariant remains module-local even though `tests/unit/workflow/test_prior_art_hardening.py:137` independently calls `snapshot_at_ref` and creates the same read-only tree. Any snapshot-producing test must leave its pytest temp tree recursively removable on macOS without weakening the production read-only snapshot contract in `src/rebar/llm/workflow/snapshot.py:_chmod_readonly`.\n\n## Approach\nMove the exact `.rebar/run_snapshots` teardown rule from the one snapshot test module into the shared pytest harness. The shared autouse fixture will act only for tests that request `tmp_path`, find snapshot-cache roots beneath that test-owned temp directory, and call the existing `rebar.llm.workflow.snapshot._rmtree_writable` cleanup seam. Remove the now-duplicate module-local fixture. This fits the defect because ownership belongs to the test harness boundary that created the ephemeral repository; it repairs every test module while avoiding a broad workflow `chmod`, production behavior changes, or a new cleanup implementation.\n\n## Scope\n- `tests/conftest.py`: centrally restore/remove read-only run-snapshot caches under each test-owned `tmp_path`.\n- `tests/unit/workflow/test_snapshot.py`: remove the superseded module-local teardown fixture.\n- `tests/unit/test_ci_snapshot_fixture_cleanup.py`: cover snapshot-producing tests from more than one module and pin recursively removable basetemps.\n\n## Testing\n- RED oracle: run the existing prior-art snapshot test in nested pytest with an explicit basetemp, assert the nested test passes, then recursively remove that basetemp; before central cleanup this fails on macOS because the second module leaves a 0555/0444 snapshot tree.\n- GREEN parity: the same behavior oracle covers both `test_snapshot_is_read_only` and `test_prior_art_hardening.py::test_snapshot_excludes_ambient_repo_state`, proving cleanup is shared rather than module-specific.\n- Negative boundary: retain assertions that production snapshots are read-only during test execution; cleanup begins only during fixture teardown and targets only `.rebar/run_snapshots` below pytest-owned `tmp_path`.\n- Run focused snapshot/cleanup and repository-isolation tests, then `make lint` and `make typecheck`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A nested pytest run of each snapshot-producing module exits 0 and its explicit basetemp is removable with ordinary `shutil.rmtree`; verified by `tests/unit/test_ci_snapshot_fixture_cleanup.py`.\n- [ ] `test_snapshot_is_read_only` still observes a non-writable snapshot before teardown; verified by the focused snapshot suite.\n- [ ] Shared cleanup is scoped to `.rebar/run_snapshots` below a test-owned `tmp_path` and the module-local duplicate is removed; verified by focused cleanup tests and code review.\n- [ ] The held-out oracle is legitimately RED before the repair, GREEN afterward, and returns to RED when the module-local-only cleanup defect is mutation-restored.\n- [ ] Focused tests, `make lint`, and `make typecheck` pass."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787027493676631001,"uuid":"33dbdb60-f18a-455d-a2bf-c1a9166fdd24"}